# gitleaks findings that are not credentials, one fingerprint per line.
#
# The fingerprint is `commit:path:rule:line`, which is the narrowest suppression gitleaks
# offers: it exempts ONE line of ONE file as introduced by ONE commit under ONE rule. A
# `[allowlist] paths` regex would exempt a whole directory forever, and a real key committed
# to that directory tomorrow would scan clean. Nothing here can do that -- edit the line and
# the fingerprint changes, and the scan goes red again.
#
# 1. A GitHub personal-access-token placeholder in Phase 5's plan, inside a test asserting
#    that the evidence redactor MASKS such a token. The document has to contain a
#    token-shaped string in order to specify redacting one. It has never been a credential:
#    its body is the digits then the lowercase alphabet, in order.
#
#    Deliberately NOT quoted here. Writing the literal string in this file made gitleaks
#    flag THIS file -- the suppression became a finding, because a text scanner cannot tell
#    an example from an instance. That is the sixth time in this repository a scanner has
#    matched the prose explaining it, so: describe the shape, never paste the sample.
5446f1a6478b0f174451b1b7d2e69340f40e8949:docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-31-phase-5-deploy-docs.md:github-pat:711

# 2. The same placeholder, as it appeared in an earlier revision of THIS file. gitleaks
#    scans history, so removing the literal from the working copy does not remove it from
#    the commit that carried it. Fingerprinted rather than history-rewritten: rewriting
#    history to erase a string that was never a credential would invalidate every other
#    fingerprint here and every commit sha quoted in the docs.
d1169754dff12f962417011b522f8cf731b44c2a:.gitleaksignore:github-pat:9
